"# When would you use RDDs, DataFrames, and Datasets?\n\nChoose the abstraction that expresses your workload clearly while preserving useful optimization opportunities. My default for a structured data pipeline is a DataFrame; I choose a typed Dataset for JVM domain logic, and an RDD when I need lower-level control.\n\n## 1. Quick decision guide\n\n| Requirement | Preferred starting point | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| SQL, joins, filtering, and aggregations | DataFrame | Expresses relational operations directly |\n| A PySpark ETL pipeline | DataFrame | Readable column expressions and SQL integration |\n| Typed Scala or Java domain objects | Dataset | Typed functional operations with encoders |\n| Custom partition-level computation | RDD, if structured APIs are unsuitable | Direct access to partition iterators |\n| A mixture of relational and typed JVM logic | DataFrame and Dataset | Switch representations where needed |\n\n## 2. Use DataFrames for structured pipelines\n\nI would start with DataFrames when building reporting tables, cleaning transaction records, joining reference data, or calculating business metrics. Named columns make the transformation intent clear. Spark SQL can use the structure of both the data and expressions when planning execution.\n\n### Example: Revenue by region — PySpark\n\nAssume `spark` is an existing SparkSession and the source contains region, status, and amount columns.\n\n```python\nfrom pyspark.sql import functions as F\n\norders = spark.read.parquet(\"s3://bucket/orders/\")\n\nrevenue = (\n    orders.filter(F.col(\"status\") == \"COMPLETED\")\n          .groupBy(\"region\")\n          .agg(F.sum(\"amount\").alias(\"total_revenue\"))\n)\n\nrevenue.show()\n```\n\nThis workload is naturally relational, so converting it into Python RDD functions would add complexity without a clear benefit.\n\n## 3. Use Datasets for typed Scala or Java logic\n\nA typed Dataset is useful when application logic works with domain objects such as employees or shipments and compile-time checking of typed operations matters. The strongly typed Dataset API is available in Scala and Java, rather than as a separate PySpark API.\n\n### Example: Typed business rule — Scala\n\n```scala\nimport spark.implicits._\n\ncase class Shipment(id: Long, weightKg: Double)\n\nval shipments = Seq(\n  Shipment(1L, 12.5),\n  Shipment(2L, 30.0)\n).toDS()\n\nval heavyShipments = shipments.filter(_.weightKg > 20.0)\nheavyShipments.show()\n```\n\nHere, a misspelled field in the typed function can be caught by the compiler. That protection does not extend to every string-based SQL expression.\n\nImportant: Typed object functions can require encoding and decoding, and their internal logic may be opaque to the SQL optimizer. Choose Datasets for useful type guarantees, rather than assuming they are always faster than DataFrames.\n\n## 4. Use RDDs for lower-level processing\n\nAn RDD is appropriate when the algorithm needs custom processing over records or partition iterators and a structured expression would be awkward. Before choosing one, check whether built-in DataFrame functions can express the operation.\n\n### Example: Custom partition-level summary — PySpark\n\nAssume `sc` is an existing SparkContext. This small example demonstrates partition-iterator access.\n\n```python\nrdd = sc.parallelize([10, 20, 30, 40], 2)\n\ndef summarize_partition(values):\n    count = 0\n    total = 0\n    for value in values:\n        count += 1\n        total += value\n    yield (count, total)\n\nsummary = rdd.mapPartitions(summarize_partition)\nprint(summary.collect())\n```\n\nEach output record summarizes one partition; it is not the global total. For an ordinary global sum or count, a DataFrame aggregation would usually be my starting point.\n\nImportant: Unstructured input alone is not a reason to choose RDDs. Text can be loaded into a DataFrame and processed using structured expressions. The deciding factor is the required computation.\n\n## 5. Practical interview clarification\n\n- In Scala, a DataFrame is `Dataset[Row]`; DataFrames and typed Datasets share Spark SQL infrastructure.\n- Converting a DataFrame to an RDD does not make arbitrary functions inside the RDD visible to Catalyst.\n- There is no universal performance ranking. Measure representative data, shuffle volume, object-processing overhead, and the final execution plan.\n- Keep conversions purposeful: use the abstraction that fits each meaningful part of the pipeline.\n\n## 6. Interview-ready answer (60–90 seconds)\n\n> I would generally start with DataFrames for structured ETL, SQL analytics, joins, and aggregations because they express the workload clearly and allow Spark SQL to optimize supported expressions.\n>\n> I would use a typed Dataset in Scala or Java when business logic benefits from domain objects and compile-time checks for typed operations. However, object encoding and opaque typed functions can introduce tradeoffs.\n>\n> I would choose an RDD when I need lower-level record or partition processing that cannot be expressed conveniently with structured APIs. I would first check whether built-in DataFrame functions meet the requirement.\n>\n> My choice depends on the workload, language, and maintainability requirements. I would validate performance rather than assume that one abstraction is always faster.\n\nKey takeaway: Start with DataFrames for relational work, use Datasets for meaningful JVM type safety, and choose RDDs when lower-level control is necessary."
